{"page": 1, "text": "Visible text of EX-99.1_2026-10-19_clf-202609308xkex991.htm, sha256 461ee483737df9c7d26bd34675cad6e958b2602fe487df0700d9aee4018ef327.\nEX-99.1 2 clf-202609308xkex991.htm EX-99.1 EXHIBIT 99.1 NEWS RELEASE Cleveland-Cliffs Reports Third-Quarter 2026 Results\nCLEVELAND\u2014October 19, 2026\u2014Cleveland-Cliffs Inc. (NYSE: CLF) today reported third-quarter results for the period ended September 30, 2026. Third-Quarter Consolidated Results \u2022Revenues of $5.6 billion \u2022Steel shipments of 4.2 million net tons \u2022GAAP net income of $165 million \u2022Adjusted EBITDA2 of $612 million \u2022GAAP net income of $0.26 per diluted share and adjusted net income1 of $0.28 per diluted share \u2022Liquidity of $3.2 billion as of September 30, 2026 Third-quarter 2026 consolidated revenues were $5.6 billion, compared to $5.2 billion in the second quarter of 2026. For the third quarter of 2026, the Company recorded GAAP net income of $165 million, or $0.26 per diluted share, with an adjusted net income1 of $0.28 per diluted share. This compares to a second quarter 2026 GAAP net loss of $134 million, or $0.25 per diluted share, with an adjusted net loss1 of $0.20 per diluted share. For the third quarter of 2026, the Company reported Adjusted EBITDA2 of $612 million, a $326 million improvement compared to Adjusted EBITDA2 of $286 million recorded in the second quarter of 2026. Cliffs\u2019 Chairman and CEO, Lourenco Goncalves, said: \u201cThird quarter was our strongest quarter in three years, with adjusted EBITDA of $612 million, a number above the $575 million guidance we provided three months ago. Average selling price, sales volumes, and unit costs all moved in the right direction relative to Q2 performance. We expect fourth quarter adjusted EBITDA to further increase, even with typical fourth-quarter seasonality and planned maintenance outages. We continued to reduce debt in Q3 and, with amplified free cash flow in Q4 and property sale proceeds, we expect a major debt reduction in the fourth quarter. With all that, we remain on track to reach our leverage target of under 2.5x net debt to EBITDA by the middle of next year.\" 1\n"}
{"page": 2, "text": "Mr. Goncalves added: \"The trade environment continues to support a healthy American steel industry. Imports remain well below historical levels, lead times are extended, and our customers increasingly recognize the value of a reliable domestic supply chain. As a result, our 2027 outlook has improved materially since our last earnings call, as several of the initiatives we identified as drivers of future earnings growth are developing more favorably than originally expected. Based on the new contracts we have already signed with our clients and prevailing index price forecasts, we would expect our 2027 adjusted EBITDA to exceed $4 billion.\" Mr. Goncalves concluded: \"Third quarter was Stelco\u2019s strongest quarter since we acquired the company two years ago. The Canadian hot-rolled market has improved substantially, turning Stelco into a positive contributor to EBITDA in Q3. However, more needs to be done by the Canadian government to correct a clearly oversupplied market for galvanized steel in Canada. As we haven\u2019t seen, and do not anticipate, any action aimed at improving the unsustainable situation for galvanized steel, we made the difficult but necessary decision to indefinitely idle our galvanizing lines at Stelco. The employees impacted by this will be brought back to work as soon as the current situation improves. In the meantime, as we concentrate on hot rolled products that actually have a market, total production tonnage at Stelco will be maintained, Stelco\u2019s contribution to Cliffs\u2019 EBITDA will further increase, and approximately 2,000 Canadian jobs are preserved.\u201d\nSteelmaking Segment Results\nThree Months Ended\nSeptember 30, Nine Months Ended\nSeptember 30, Three Months Ended\n2026 2025 2026 2025 Jun. 30, 2026\nExternal Sales Volumes - In Thousands\nSteel Products (net tons) 4,150 4,029 12,283 12,459 4,025\nSelling Price - Per Net Ton\nAverage net selling price per net ton of steel products $ 1,177 $ 1,032 $ 1,117 $ 1,009 $ 1,124\nOperating Results - In Millions\nRevenues $ 5,447 $ 4,561 $ 15,256 $ 13,799 $ 5,052\nCash cost of goods sold (4,762) (4,356) (14,086) (13,606) (4,703)\nCash margin 685 205 1,170 193 349\nDepreciation, depletion, and amortization (231) (261) (698) (882) (236)\nGross margin $ 454 $ (56) $ 472 $ (689) $ 113\nThird-quarter 2026 steel product sales volumes of 4.2 million net tons consisted of 46% hot-rolled, 31% coated, 15% cold-rolled, 4% plate, and 4% stainless and electrical. 2\n"}
{"page": 3, "text": "Steelmaking revenues of $5.4 billion included $1.8 billion, or 34%, of sales to the distributors and converters market; $1.5 billion, or 28%, of direct sales to the automotive market; $1.5 billion, or 28%, of sales to the infrastructure and manufacturing market; and $532 million, or 10%, of sales to steel producers. Liquidity and Cash Flow As of September 30, 2026, the Company had $3.2 billion in total liquidity. Outlook The Company expects fourth-quarter 2026 adjusted EBITDA2 to be approximately $715 million, an increase from the third quarter as a result of increased pricing, partially offset by seasonality, increased maintenance outages, input costs and incentive compensation accruals. Based on 2027 contract changes and prevailing price forecasts, the Company would expect 2027 adjusted EBITDA to exceed $4 billion. Additional outlook details can be found on page 10 of the earnings presentation published this morning on clevelandcliffs.com/investors. Conference Call Cleveland-Cliffs Inc. will host a conference call this morning, October 19, 2026, at 8:30 a.m. ET. The call will be broadcast live and archived on Cliffs' website: www.clevelandcliffs.com. About Cleveland-Cliffs Inc. Cleveland-Cliffs is a leading North America-based steel producer with focus on value-added sheet products, particularly for the automotive industry. The Company is vertically integrated from the mining of iron ore, production of pellets and direct reduced iron, and processing of ferrous scrap through primary steelmaking and downstream finishing, stamping, tooling, and tubing. Headquartered in Cleveland, Ohio, Cleveland-Cliffs employs approximately 25,000 people across its operations in the United States and Canada. 3\n"}
